# Specs / SpecRow — a list of key-value facts. Version numbers, limits, defaults,
# "what this thing is" tables: the block a reader scans rather than reads.
#
#     use Specs():
#         use SpecRow("Language", "Rust, edition 2024")
#         use SpecRow("Storage", "Write-ahead log with recovery")
#         use SpecRow("Auth", "Token, per-tenant")
#
# WHY IT IS NOT A TABLE AND NOT A `Grid2`. A table is for data with more than one
# axis, and this has one: a name and its value. A two-column grid gets the columns
# right at a comfortable width and wrong at every other one — the value column ends
# up as narrow as the longest key, or the key column as wide as the longest value.
# The rule here is `grid-template-columns: minmax(9rem, 22ch) 1fr` per ROW, so keys
# align down the block, a long key takes the space it needs up to a cap, and the
# whole thing folds to one column per row when the container gets narrow, which
# keeps a spec list readable inside a 320px rail.
#
# THE ROWS ARE COMPONENTS AND THE CONTAINER IS A SLOT, which is this library's
# usual shape and it matters here for the reason `layout/grid.fct` gives at
# length: a spec list built from a query is `for f in Fact … class "x-specs"`,
# with the loop AS the container, because a `for` dropped into this slot renders
# as one grid cell holding every row. The class vocabulary is reachable without
# the wrapper for exactly that case.
#
# PROMOTED FROM A SITE. f33d3r.com wrote `SpecRow`/`Specs` for the "what this
# layer is" block on four pages, and nominated it as the most generally useful
# thing it had written — correctly: a definition list is not a site's idea, it is
# a shape every documentation page and every settings summary needs, and every
# repository that lacks one grows a slightly different one. The shape is promoted;
# the site's own type scale is not, and the rules below name no font and no colour
# beyond the muted token the host already defines for secondary text.
app FacetUI:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-specs { display: flex; flex-direction: column;
                  border: none; background: none; padding: 0; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-spec { display: grid; gap: 4px 20px; align-items: baseline;
                 grid-template-columns: minmax(9rem, 22ch) 1fr; padding: 10px 0;
                 border: none; background: none; }
        # THE DIVIDER STEPS THROUGH THE WRAPPER. `.x-spec + .x-spec` is what this
        # rule used to say, and it never matched anything: `use SpecRow(...)`
        # renders an element of its own around each row, so two rows are a
        # wrapper's child and the next wrapper's child — never DOM siblings — and
        # every list of specs rendered with no rules between the rows at all. `+`
        # and `:last-child` walk the DOM, and `display: contents` does not help
        # because it moves boxes, not selectors; the only fix is to compare the
        # WRAPPERS and then descend. `> * + *` does exactly that and does not care
        # whether the wrapper is a `.fa-use` or an unclassed region div, nor how
        # many levels of composition sit between it and the `.x-spec`.
        #
        # It does require the rows to be direct children of the `.x-specs` element.
        # `use Specs():` with `use SpecRow(...)` inside is that; a `for` that
        # renders the rows must carry the class ITSELF — `for s in Spec class
        # "x-specs"` — for the same reason `data/table.fct` makes the loop the row
        # group rather than putting a loop inside one.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-specs > * + * .x-spec { border-top: 1px solid var(--x-line, #2f3336); }
        # A `text` node is a span, so the two halves say `display: block` or the
        # grid places two inline boxes it cannot align.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-spec-k { display: block; color: var(--fa-muted); font-size: 14px; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-spec-v { display: block; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.5; }
        # One column per row below the point where two would leave the value four
        # words wide. Container width is not queryable here, so this is the one
        # media query in the file and it guards the case that is unreadable rather
        # than the case that is merely tight.
        @media (max-width: 480px) {
            [data-fa-mount] .x-spec { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
        }
    component Specs():
        box class "x-specs":
            slot
    component SpecRow(key: text, value: text):
        box class "x-spec":
            text "{key}" class "x-spec-k"
            text "{value}" class "x-spec-v"
